//
// A NAMED REST LEAVES ROOM FOR THE FIXED ELEMENTS AFTER IT (`@FR-P-Rest`, with `(P-Point)`,
// loft#1419).  `..name` binds `src[i .. len - t]`, `t` counting the fixed patterns after it — a variant
// or a literal among them — so elements may follow it and the middle is what it captures.  Two
// sections, each with its cells and receipt.
//
// ── a-named-rest-leaves-room-for-the-elements-after-it ──
//
// @FR-P-Rest — `..name` binds a FRESH vector holding `src[i .. len - t]`, where `t` counts the
// fixed elements AFTER the rest.  So elements may follow a named rest, and the captured middle
// shrinks by however many do.
//
// The rule said that and the parser refused it: `[a, ..mid, z]` reported *"a named rest `..mid`
// must be the last slice element"*.  What makes the refusal the defect rather than the rule
// over-reaching is the CONTROL below — `[a, .., z]`, the same shape with the middle un-named,
// has always been legal and has always bound `z` from the end.  Only naming the middle was
// refused, so the language could match the shape but not say what was in it.
//
// Nothing had to be built to close it.  The arm's length gate is `head + tail <= len`, the tail
// elements are read at negative indices, and `hi = len - tail_len` was already being computed in
// the refused branch — the diagnostic sat in front of a correct lowering.  Every cell here is
// therefore also a claim about the un-named form, which is why both spellings are asserted side
// by side on the same subjects: if they ever disagree, one of the two paths has drifted.
//
// ⚠ The empty middle and the under-length subject are the cells that matter, not the roomy one.
// `[a, ..mid, z]` on a 2-element subject must MATCH with `mid` empty (`lo == hi`), and on a
// 1-element subject must NOT match at all — `fixed` is 2.  A guard that only ever feeds it five
// elements passes on a lowering that computed `hi` as `len` and swallowed the tail.
//
// The struct and struct-enum cells guard `(H-Alloc)`: the middle is a fresh, independent vector,
// so mutating it must leave the subject intact, and matching the SAME subject twice must answer
// the same thing.  That reuse cell is the store-lifetime class `35c-rest-capture.loft` records —
// a materialised sub-slice that frees a view of the subject's own records reads correct once and
// wrong the second time.
//
// A tail element is a BARE NAME in both spellings; a variant sub-pattern or literal after a `..`
// is a parse-error cascade (loft#1419), which is the part of `t` still outstanding.
//
// @falsified-at: 4e5725a2c — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0 (`make falsify`, both
//   backends, a full build of that commit).  There every arm below is refused outright with
//   "a named rest `..mid` must be the last slice element", so the file never reaches `main` —
//   the channel that moves is the EXIT code, not an assertion count.
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/a-named-rest-leaves-room-for-the-elements-after-it.patch
//   — the same channels as the control above.  That control is reachable from no public
//   ref, so the patch is the runnable form of this receipt: it reintroduces the fault on
//   top of HEAD.  Score it with `scripts/falsify.sh <this file> --patch <that patch>`.
//   The channels matching are what say the patch reintroduces THIS defect, not a neighbour.
//   HOLDS: the assertion channel does NOT move — the cells read the same on both
//   trees — along with leak, panic, free-refusal and expectations.  The exit channel
//   carries this one, so a patch that moves an assertion is reintroducing something
//   else and must be rejected.
//
// ── 1419-a-fixed-pattern-after-a-rest-is-a-tail-element ──
//
// @FR-P-Rest defines a named rest as `src[i .. len-t]` with `t` the count of FIXED patterns
// after the rest, and `(P-Point)` makes a variant or a literal one of those patterns.  So a
// variant after a `..` is a fixed pattern the rules admit — and the parser took only a BARE
// NAME there, so `End { e }` bound the name `End` and then choked on `{`, four cascading
// "Expect token" messages naming nothing the author could act on (loft#1419).
//
// The reason it was bare-name-only is worth keeping: a tail element reads from the END, at
// `-(tail_len - j)`, and a bare name defers trivially because the bind loop runs after `]`.
// A variant sub-pattern or a literal emits its test AS it parses, so it needs its index in
// hand — which is not known until the whole tail has been seen.  Counting the tail once, when
// the `..` is consumed, gives every branch the index it needs, and the tail then admits the
// same element forms the head does.
//
// The head cells are controls: they exercise the branches that already worked, so a cure that
// merely moved the head's positions would fail them rather than read green.
//
// @falsified-at: d9850164 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0, assertion column 0 on
//   both sides.  Zero because the control build does not PARSE the file: the tail sub-pattern
//   cascades and no cell runs to fail.  An exit-only move is the weaker falsification, which is
//   why the head controls and the two-fixed-tail cell are here — they assert VALUES, so a cure
//   that parsed the tail but read the wrong positions fails on a number rather than passing.
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees.
//   Falsify prints only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of
//   those is clean on both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.

enum Tok {
  Kw { word: text },
  Num { value: integer },
}
struct P {
  x: integer,
  y: integer,
}

// ── head 1 / tail 1, and the un-named CONTROL on the same subjects ───────────
fn named(v: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  match v { [a, ..mid, z] => a * 10000 + len(mid) * 100 + z, _ => -1 }
}
fn gap(v: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  match v { [a, .., z] => a * 10000 + z, _ => -1 }
}
// the middle's CONTENTS, not just its length
fn mid_first(v: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  match v { [_, ..mid, _] => mid[0], _ => -1 }
}
fn mid_last(v: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  match v { [_, ..mid, _] => mid[len(mid) - 1] ?? -1, _ => -1 }
}

// ── head 0 / tail 2 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
fn no_head(v: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  match v { [..mid, y, z] => len(mid) * 1000 + y * 10 + z, _ => -1 }
}

// ── head 2 / tail 2 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
fn h2t2(v: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  match v { [a, b, ..mid, y, z] => a * 100000 + b * 10000 + len(mid) * 1000 + y * 10 + z, _ => -1 }
}

// ── tail-only rest still means what it always did (t == 0) ───────────────────
fn tail_only(v: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  match v { [a, ..rest] => a * 100 + len(rest), _ => -1 }
}

// ── heap element type ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
fn text_mid(v: vector<text>) -> text {
  match v { [a, ..mid, z] => "{a}/{len(mid)}/{z}", _ => "none" }
}
fn text_mid_first(v: vector<text>) -> text {
  match v { [_, ..mid, _] => mid[0], _ => "none" }
}

// ── independence: the middle is a fresh vector (H-Alloc) ─────────────────────
fn struct_mid(v: vector<P>) -> vector<P> {
  match v { [_, ..mid, _] => mid, _ => [] }
}

// ── struct-enum: a captured head field escapes, with a rest AND a tail ───────
fn ntoks(ts: vector<Tok>) -> integer {
  len(ts)
}
fn first_word(ts: vector<Tok>) -> text {
  match ts { [Kw { word }, ..rhs, _] => "{word}/{ntoks(rhs)}", _ => "none" }
}

// ── a guard rejects the arm; the next one still binds the middle correctly ───
fn guarded(v: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  match v {
    [a, ..mid, _] if a > 100 => a + len(mid),
    [a, ..mid, _] => a * 100 + len(mid),
    _ => -1,
  }
}

fn head_1_tail_1_leaves_the_middle_between_them() {
  // roomy: mid = v[1 .. len-1]
  assert(named([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) == 10305, "named five: {named([1,2,3,4,5])}");
  assert(mid_first([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) == 2, "mid[0]: {mid_first([1,2,3,4,5])}");
  assert(mid_last([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) == 4, "mid[-1]: {mid_last([1,2,3,4,5])}");
  // ⚠ empty middle — lo == hi, and the arm must still MATCH
  assert(named([1, 2]) == 10002, "named two (empty mid): {named([1,2])}");
  // ⚠ under-length — `fixed` is 2, so neither element may double as both
  assert(named([1]) == -1, "named one must not match: {named([1])}");
  assert(named([]) == -1, "named empty must not match: {named([])}");

  // CONTROL: the un-named gap agrees on every one of those subjects
  assert(gap([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) == 10005, "gap five: {gap([1,2,3,4,5])}");
  assert(gap([1, 2]) == 10002, "gap two: {gap([1,2])}");
  assert(gap([1]) == -1, "gap one must not match: {gap([1])}");
  assert(gap([]) == -1, "gap empty must not match: {gap([])}");
}

fn the_middle_shrinks_by_however_many_elements_follow_it() {
  // head 0 / tail 2
  assert(no_head([1, 2, 3, 4]) == 2034, "no_head four: {no_head([1,2,3,4])}");
  assert(no_head([1, 2]) == 12, "no_head two (empty mid): {no_head([1,2])}");
  assert(no_head([1]) == -1, "no_head one must not match: {no_head([1])}");

  // head 2 / tail 2
  assert(h2t2([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]) == 122056, "h2t2 six: {h2t2([1,2,3,4,5,6])}");
  assert(h2t2([1, 2, 3, 4]) == 120034, "h2t2 four (empty mid): {h2t2([1,2,3,4])}");
  assert(h2t2([1, 2, 3]) == -1, "h2t2 three must not match: {h2t2([1,2,3])}");

  // t == 0 is unchanged
  assert(tail_only([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) == 104, "tail_only: {tail_only([1,2,3,4,5])}");
  assert(tail_only([9]) == 900, "tail_only single: {tail_only([9])}");
}

fn a_middle_of_heap_elements_is_its_own_vector() {
  assert(
    text_mid(["a", "b", "c", "d"]) == "a/2/d",
    "text mid: {text_mid([\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\"])}"
  );
  assert(
    text_mid_first(["a", "b", "c", "d"]) == "b",
    "text mid[0]: {text_mid_first([\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\"])}"
  );
  // an empty middle still MATCHES — `fixed` is 2 and the subject has 2
  assert(text_mid(["a", "b"]) == "a/0/b", "text empty mid: {text_mid([\"a\",\"b\"])}");

  // (H-Alloc) — mutating the middle must not reach the subject
  src = [
    P { x: 10, y: 1 },
    P { x: 20, y: 2 },
    P { x: 30, y: 3 },
    P { x: 40, y: 4 },
  ];
  m = struct_mid(src);
  assert(len(m) == 2 && m[0].x == 20 && m[1].x == 30, "struct mid: {m[0].x},{m[1].x}");
  m[0].x = 99;
  assert(m[0].x == 99, "struct mid mutated: {m[0].x}");
  assert(src[1].x == 20, "subject intact after mutating the middle: {src[1].x}");
}

fn matching_the_same_subject_twice_answers_the_same_thing() {
  ts = [
    Kw { word: "let" },
    Num { value: 1 },
    Num { value: 2 },
    Num { value: 3 },
  ];
  // the captured head field escapes as a text while a rest AND a tail are present
  assert(first_word(ts) == "let/2", "struct-enum rest+tail: {first_word(ts)}");
  // ⚠ the reuse cell — a materialised middle that frees a view of the subject's own
  // records answers correctly the first time and wrongly the second
  assert(
    first_word(ts) == "let/2" && first_word(ts) == "let/2",
    "struct-enum reuse (subject intact): {first_word(ts)}"
  );
  assert(first_word([
    Num { value: 9 },
    Num { value: 8 },
  ]) == "none", "struct-enum fallthrough");
}

fn a_failed_guard_leaves_the_middle_to_the_next_arm() {
  assert(guarded([200, 1, 2, 3]) == 202, "guard taken: {guarded([200,1,2,3])}");
  assert(guarded([5, 1, 2, 3]) == 502, "guard rejected, next arm binds: {guarded([5,1,2,3])}");
  assert(guarded([7]) == -1, "guard, under-length: {guarded([7])}");
}

fn test_a_named_rest_leaves_room_for_the_elements_after_it() {
  head_1_tail_1_leaves_the_middle_between_them();
  the_middle_shrinks_by_however_many_elements_follow_it();
  a_middle_of_heap_elements_is_its_own_vector();
  matching_the_same_subject_twice_answers_the_same_thing();
  a_failed_guard_leaves_the_middle_to_the_next_arm();
  print("a-named-rest-leaves-room-for-the-elements-after-it: PASS");
}

enum S2Tok { S2Kw { word: text }, End { e: integer }, S2Num { n: integer } }

fn tail_variant(ts: vector<S2Tok>) -> integer {
  match ts { [S2Kw { word }, .., End { e }] => e, _ => -1 }
}
fn tail_named_rest(ts: vector<S2Tok>) -> integer {
  match ts { [S2Kw { word }, ..mid, End { e }] => e + len(mid), _ => -1 }
}
fn tail_two_fixed(ts: vector<S2Tok>) -> integer {
  match ts { [S2Kw { word }, .., S2Num { n }, End { e }] => n * 100 + e, _ => -1 }
}
fn tail_bare_name(ts: vector<S2Tok>) -> integer {
  match ts { [S2Kw { word }, .., z] => match z { End { e } => e, _ => 0 }, _ => -1 }
}
// `_` as a tail element.  Accepted BEFORE this fix as well — `(P-Point)` admits it and the
// entry grouped it with the bare binding — so this is a CONTROL, not a cell: it says the cure
// admitted the two new forms without disturbing the two that already worked.  It is here
// because `formal/matching.md`'s `(P-Rest)` conformance line names all four, and a conformance
// claim nothing runs is a claim nobody re-measures.
fn tail_underscore(ns: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  match ns { [a, .., _] => a, _ => -1 }
}
fn head_variant(ts: vector<S2Tok>) -> integer {
  match ts { [End { e }, ..] => e, _ => -1 }
}
fn literal_in_tail(ns: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  match ns { [a, .., 9] => a, _ => -1 }
}
fn literal_in_head(ns: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  match ns { [9, .., a] => a, _ => -1 }
}

fn an_underscore_tail_still_matches() {
  assert_eq("{tail_underscore([1, 2, 3, 9])}", "1", "`_` as a tail element still binds the head");
  assert_eq("{tail_underscore([1, 9])}", "1", "and with an empty middle");
  assert_eq("{tail_underscore([1])}", "-1", "and needs the tail element to exist");
}

fn a_variant_after_a_rest_is_a_tail_element() {
  assert_eq("{tail_variant([S2Kw{word:\"a\"}, End{e:9}])}", "9", "the tail variant binds with an empty middle");
  assert_eq("{tail_variant([S2Kw{word:\"a\"}, S2Num{n:3}, End{e:9}])}", "9", "and with a middle");
  assert_eq("{tail_variant([S2Kw{word:\"a\"}, S2Num{n:3}])}", "-1", "and does not match a wrong tail variant");
}

// The rest is `len - t` long, so naming it proves `t` was counted: one fixed tail element
// leaves the middle one shorter than a bare `..` would.
fn a_named_rest_shrinks_by_the_fixed_tail() {
  assert_eq("{tail_named_rest([S2Kw{word:\"a\"}, End{e:9}])}", "9", "an empty middle adds nothing");
  assert_eq("{tail_named_rest([S2Kw{word:\"a\"}, S2Num{n:3}, End{e:9}])}", "10", "a one-element middle adds one");
  assert_eq("{tail_named_rest([S2Kw{word:\"a\"}, S2Num{n:3}, S2Num{n:4}, End{e:9}])}", "11", "a two-element middle adds two");
}

// Two fixed tail patterns: each must read its OWN position from the end, so a cure that
// counted them but mis-assigned the indices answers a different number rather than failing.
fn two_fixed_tail_patterns_read_their_own_positions() {
  assert_eq("{tail_two_fixed([S2Kw{word:\"a\"}, S2Num{n:3}, S2Num{n:4}, End{e:9}])}", "409",
            "the LAST S2Num is the one the pattern names");
  assert_eq("{tail_two_fixed([S2Kw{word:\"a\"}, End{e:9}])}", "-1", "too short does not match");
}

fn a_literal_after_a_rest_is_a_tail_element() {
  assert_eq("{literal_in_tail([1, 2, 9])}", "1", "the tail literal matches");
  assert_eq("{literal_in_tail([1, 2, 8])}", "-1", "and refuses a different value");
}

// ── Controls: the head forms, which already worked ──
fn the_head_forms_are_unchanged() {
  assert_eq("{tail_bare_name([S2Kw{word:\"a\"}, End{e:9}])}", "9", "a bare tail name still binds the last element");
  assert_eq("{head_variant([End{e:5}, S2Kw{word:\"z\"}])}", "5", "a head variant still matches");
  assert_eq("{head_variant([S2Kw{word:\"a\"}, End{e:9}])}", "-1", "and still refuses the wrong head");
  assert_eq("{literal_in_head([9, 2, 7])}", "7", "a head literal still matches");
  assert_eq("{literal_in_head([1, 2, 7])}", "-1", "and still refuses");
}
